.TH SIMPLE_SHELL 1 "April 2026" "1.0" "Simple Shell Manual"

.SH NAME
simple_shell \- a simple UNIX command line interpreter

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hsh
[\fIscript\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a minimal UNIX command line interpreter (shell).
It reads commands from standard input one per line and executes them.
Commands are located by searching each directory listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable, or may be specified as absolute or relative paths.

In interactive mode a prompt
.B ($)
is displayed before each command.
In non-interactive mode commands are read from a pipe or file without a prompt.

.SH INVOCATION
If standard input is a terminal,
.B hsh
runs in interactive mode and displays a prompt.
Otherwise it reads commands silently (non-interactive mode).

.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
.TP
.B exit
Exit the shell. The exit status is the status of the last executed command.
.TP
.B env
Print all environment variables, one per line, in the form
.IR NAME=value .

.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
For each command line,
.B hsh
tokenises the input on whitespace to produce a command name and its arguments.

If the command name contains a slash it is used as a direct path.
Otherwise
.B hsh
searches each directory in
.B PATH
in order and executes the first match found.

If no executable is found the shell prints an error to standard error:

.RS
.nf
hsh: \fIline_number\fR: \fIcommand\fR: not found
.fi
.RE

and returns exit status 127.

.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of the shell is the exit status of the last command executed.
Exit status 127 means the command was not found.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Colon-separated list of directories searched for executables.

.SH EXAMPLES
Interactive mode:

.RS
.nf
$ ./hsh
($) ls
hsh  shell.c  shell.h
($) /bin/echo hello
hello
($) exit
$
.fi
.RE

Non-interactive mode:

.RS
.nf
$ echo "ls" | ./hsh
hsh  shell.c  shell.h
$ echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
hsh  shell.c  shell.h
.fi
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR execve (2),
.BR fork (2),
.BR waitpid (2),
.BR getenv (3)

